 ## Usage
 
 ```python
-from accounts.reset import request_reset
+from accounts.reset import complete_reset, request_reset
 
 token = request_reset("user@example.com")
+
+# ... token is emailed to the user out of band ...
+
+ok = complete_reset("user@example.com", token, "new-password")
 ```
 
 Password storage and token comparison live in `accounts.crypto`, which the reset
-flow calls rather than hashing or comparing anything itself. Issuing a token and
-checking one are implemented; completing a reset is not.
+flow calls rather than hashing or comparing anything itself.
+
+## Reset flow
+
+1. **Request.** `request_reset(email)` generates a token with `secrets.token_urlsafe`
+   and records it for the account together with the time it was issued. More than
+   one token can be outstanding for an account at once (for example if the user
+   asks for a new email because the first didn't arrive) — each is tracked
+   independently.
+2. **Check (optional).** `check_token(email, token)` reports whether a token is
+   currently valid for an account, without consuming it or changing any state.
+   This is useful for validating a token as soon as a user opens the reset link,
+   before asking them to choose a new password.
+3. **Complete.** `complete_reset(email, token, new_password)` verifies the token
+   and, if it is valid, hashes and stores `new_password` via `accounts.crypto`.
+   A token is valid only if it was issued for that account, has not already been
+   used, and is being redeemed within `TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS` (30 minutes) of when it
+   was issued. When the token is unknown, expired, or already used,
+   `complete_reset` returns `False` rather than raising, so callers can show a
+   generic "link is invalid or expired" message without distinguishing the cause.
+4. **Invalidation.** On a successful reset, every outstanding token for that
+   account is cleared — not just the one that was redeemed. This makes tokens
+   single use and also closes out any other reset request still in flight for the
+   account, so an old reset link can't be used after the password has already
+   been changed.
